[decoded] "The sky appears blue during the day due to a phenomenon called Rayleigh scattering. Sunlight contains all colors of the visible spectrum, but shorter wavelengths like blue scatter much more easily than longer wavelengths like red when they hit Earth's atmosphere. Since the atmosphere is relatively thin, this scattered blue light reaches our eyes from all directions, making the sky look blue.\n\nAt sunset, the sun is lower on the horizon, so sunlight must travel through a much thicker layer of the atmosphere to reach us. During this longer journey, most of the blue light is scattered away completely. The remaining light, dominated by longer wavelengths like red, orange, and yellow, passes through to our eyes. This concentrated path of red light creates the vibrant colors seen at sunrise and sunset. Essentially, the angle of the sun changes how much light interacts with the air, shifting the perceived color from blue to red."
